From: Azure Linux <azurelinux@microsoft.com>
Date: Wed, 04 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ghmac: route GHmac through OpenSSL instead of GnuTLS

Azure Linux variant of the Fedora/RHEL gnutls-hmac.patch series, with
the crypto backend switched from GnuTLS to OpenSSL.

The Fedora/RHEL patch series routes GLib's GHmac primitives through
GnuTLS so that GHmac honours the system FIPS policy. In Azure Linux
the system reference crypto provider is OpenSSL (the crypto-policies
package ships openssl_fips.config), so routing GHmac through GnuTLS
forces a separate crypto stack (gnutls + nettle + gmp + libidn2 +
libunistring) into the runtime image solely to back GLib's HMAC API.

This patch swaps the backend to libcrypto.so.3 (still loaded via
dlopen so libglib has only a weak runtime dependency, mirroring the
Fedora pattern). It is equivalent to applying the four upstream
commits below, but with ghmac-gnutls.c replaced by an ghmac-openssl.c
that uses the OpenSSL legacy HMAC API (still exported from
libcrypto.so.3, routes through the FIPS provider).

Combines and replaces the four upstream commits:
  [PATCH 1/4] ghmac: Split off wrapper functions into ghmac-utils.c
  [PATCH 2/4] Add a gnutls backend for GHmac
  [PATCH 3/4] dlopen GnuTLS instead of linking directly
  [PATCH 4/4] Disable MD5 and SHA-1 HMac tests
---
